# Line endings — deterministic across Windows, macOS, Linux, and Docker.
#
# Why this file exists: this project runs a Python worker and Postgres inside
# Linux containers. A shell script saved with Windows line endings (CRLF) fails
# inside a Linux container with "bad interpreter: /bin/bash^M" — an error that
# is genuinely hard to diagnose because the file looks completely normal.
#
# Forcing LF everywhere removes that whole class of bug before it can happen.
# Windows editors (VS Code, modern Notepad) handle LF files without any issue.

# Default: treat as text, store and check out with LF.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Must be LF — these run inside Linux containers.
*.sh        text eol=lf
*.bash      text eol=lf
Dockerfile  text eol=lf
*.dockerfile text eol=lf
*.yml       text eol=lf
*.yaml      text eol=lf
*.py        text eol=lf
*.sql       text eol=lf

# Must be CRLF — these only ever run on Windows.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf
*.ps1 text eol=crlf

# Binary — never convert, never diff as text.
*.docx binary
*.pdf  binary
*.png  binary
*.jpg  binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif  binary
*.webp binary
*.ico  binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf  binary
*.otf  binary
*.zip  binary

# Lockfiles: keep them out of diffs so reviews stay readable.
package-lock.json -diff
